e are again  reliving those memories because another  sovereign has passed on it must be quite  something for you personally  it is something for me personally having  worked for her for 12 years and having  been i'm a monarchist i believe in the  monarchy i don't think there's any other  alternative with a queen or a king like  the queen 70 years on the throne it was  continuity she had respect not just the  uk but global respect global respect in  her realm states like canada queen of  canada  did you take us back to uh  what it might have been like what you  think it might have been like in the  palace when it became clear that the  queen  was dying or perhaps it already died  well the palace don't panic  one thing um it was very clear that the  queen  uh was not a hundred percent well not a  hundred percent fit  and it wasn't a question of if but when  so plans would have been taken out of  the  dust blown off  and they would have start making  arrangements  we can hear the work going on right near  us now we're right near the houses of  parliament this whole area i expect will  be flooded with people right for the  lying in state people will be so anxious  to try to pay their respects  well king george the sixth in 1952 was  well over 400 000 people i would have  thought probably  if they manage it properly um that in  probably in excess of a million people  will go through in the uh in the four  days  everybody will want to go if go judging  by what's been happening at buckingham  palace people most certainly will want  to go and pay their respects because it  is history it is living history as you  watch the transition from  prince charles to king charles iii what  did you what did you make of  how he his demeanor how he  has handled things at least from a  public perspective the king got  everything  absolutely 101 right  back in london  the car stopped short of the north  central gate of buckingham palace he and  the queen consort got out they did a  walkabout they talked to mourners  they were very relaxed they were smiling  and it was striking just the right chord  he is a man of the people he has always  been a man of the people so what he did  outside buckingham palace was absolutely  spot on when he went in  and he gave his television address that  too was pitch perfect  he said all the right things he felt  them  he talked about his mama  he talked about her  joining his his beloved father  so  it was a perfect address  so yeah he is getting stuck in and doing  what is to be done so the transition is  is smooth  that's that's how the monarchy works  there isn't a gap the queen is dead long  live the king that's how it works the  royal standard never comes down because  the sovereign is always alive  dickie arbiter thank you thank you  next the priceless story behind the  queen's portrait you probably have in  your wallet  [Music]  the queen was the most photographed  woman in the world captured on iphones  and by official photographers most often  during formal events but in intimate  moments with her family too and her  image of course is everywhere on money  stamps and countless other items across  the commonwealth so what happens to all  that now  jeff semple went to find out  her face is perhaps the most  photographed in history  and some of the most iconic images of  queen elizabeth ii were snapped by ian  jones  over 30 years the royal photographer  captured countless moments from around  the world but one image in particular is  distinctly canadian so canadians know  this picture very well yes they may not  know that you took this photograph  possibly not no they know who's on it  in 2012 jones was invited to buckingham  palace to take a photo of the queen for  canada's new twenty dollar bill but when  the queen arrived jones realized the  office chair they'd prepared was much  too tall so when she sat down in the  chair her toes just about to touch the  floor  um and  the tip-toe it's a little skip around so  she ended up rotating on her toes with  this glint in her eye and she thought it  was absolutely hilarious but what it did  prompt was a wonderful glint and a smile  and that really set the tone for the  portrait session the queen's image and  mark are ubiquitous found on everything  from post boxes and stamps to flags and  t-tins and the ascension of a new  monarch will now require a major royal  rebranding  the british national anthem has now  changed back to god save the king new  currency featuring charles's portrait  will be produced after his coronation  but it'll likely be a while before he  gives his mother a run for her money the  queen's image is featured on coins  across the commonwealth at one time she  appeared on 33 different currencies the  most of any monarch we have a series of  coins over her record reign her image  was updated five times charting her  evolution as sovereign so now for the  first time ever in most of our lives  we're going to see more than one monarch  on the coins of the realm and the same  goes for stamps this profile first  issued in 1966 has been reproduced 320  billion times more than any artwork in  history the uk will now stop producing  these stamps but like the queen's  currency they'll still be accepted they  will fall out of circulation slowly but  i imagine the coins of elizabeth will be  circulating for many decades to come her  reign is over but will carry her with us  for years to come jeff semple global  news london  that is global national for this monday  from overlooking westminster abbey in  london i'm donna friesen preparations  are well underway here for the state  funeral on monday that will attract  leaders from all over the world and it's  expected a multitude of other people  thanks for watching hope to see you here  again tomorrow bye  [Music]  you
 

